s  pretty uh pretty impressive um and to my  right miriam francois who's a political  commentator theorist and uh broadcaster  focuses on on islam and feminism and the  middle east so um i'm looking forward to  hearing what they have to say on the  subject and i'm going to ask them to  begin  um uh miriam perhaps uh you could uh  begin to and  tell us uh to address the the theme of  the day the debate in four minutes  sisters and sisterhood in four minutes  are the female elite threatening the  sisterhoods um  i would probably want to start out by  unpicking a lot of the terms in that so  um who who gets to define who this elite  are how are we defining success  um  are we taken over now within discourses  on feminism by  um ideas that are being defined by a  global capitalist system according to  which essentially the measure of  someone's worth is their productive  capability within this broader system  and the extent to which that comes to  define our priorities within feminism  so the elite are these quote unquote  successful women running big companies  and earning lots of money  but essentially you could flip it in  another way and say that uh  they're part of a very small elite that  uh requires the exploitation of a large  number of people male and female uh in  order to accrue an increasingly  significant amount of wealth  towards an increasingly small number of  people and so i'm not sure that they'd  be my elite or my notion of what would  be success  and then when it comes to the notion of  sisterhood  i find the term a little bit fluffy  personally  i prefer the term solidarity which i  think has much more  implications for notions of mutuality  accountability  and recognitions of common interests i  would even want to unpick the notion  that women as a category  are  necessarily bound by shared experiences  i think even within the same societies  women  experience  very different versions  of  quote unquote equality or inequality  prejudice  and so  it's therefore difficult to talk about  shared struggles  uh in any meaningful sense without  looking at the broader  power relations within which individual  women find themselves  and so more funny to just maybe end on  this note i'm not personally that  interested i'm not saying it's an  irrelevant discussion i think it's  important to talk about the struggles of  like the one percent of the one percent  but i'm probably much more interested in  the extent to which the one percent of  the one percent are contributing to  deepening  worsening economic precarity for a large  number of people male and female and  amongst that group of men and women  who have very different experiences it  should be said and that the developing  world or the global south is very  differentiated it shouldn't be presented  as one group  but within that women tend to suffer the  hard wedge of it  well i guess you know  in theory  i'm part of this elite right because i'm  a woman i've been a ceo  i have in conventional corporate terms  been very successful  i'm extremely  unhappy  about that  i mean i was reflecting before we came  in this morning  of  an experience i had when i was a senior  executive producer at the bbc and my  boss  put his arm around my shoulder because  you could do that in those days  and said good old margaret she's one of  the chaps  and he meant it as a compliment and i  think at the time  i probably took it as a compliment  and i can also remember when i was  working in the united states i was  running vc backed software companies  um my lead investor  thinking i'd been off on vacation  because he'd completely failed to notice  i was pregnant  so i was clearly doing a fantastic job  of being a guy  and and i'd have to say this doesn't  fill me with pride it fills me with  horror  because i think the truth is that as  marvelous as it is to see women at the  top of organizations  the cost is very high  i think the cost is fundamentally not  always and with differing degrees but  nevertheless the cost is one  of assimilation  and you know to be absolutely honest i  didn't come into business to maintain  the status quo  and i didn't want to get the power to  run organizations  to perpetuate the status quo i always  thought the whole point  of being a marginalized outside group  was to come in and shake things up  and i still think that  i still think the way most of our  organizations run are run  is inept unkind unfair  cruel wasteful and unproductive  and i think that those of us who are on  the outside probably see that more  clearly than those who are in on the  inside  i also think  trickle down doesn't work in any context  it doesn't work economically  it doesn't work in sport having a couple  of olympic athletes has not increased  sports participation or the health of  the nation  and trickle-down doesn't work  when it comes to  women to ethnic minorities to any  marginalized group having a couple of  figureheads at the top it's great having  the role models and saying it can be  done  but it doesn't move  it doesn't redefine the norm in any  meaningful way  and i think we also experienced that  under mrs thatcher who was you know a  role model of female leadership that was  highly assimilated to male norms and did  absolutely nothing for women i think  this target of 30 percent of board  members being female will do nothing to  change the way our corporations  are run or managed i think we need a  much deeper  more grass roots  movement if we're going to effect any  change and i'm absolutely mortified and  disheartened by the fact that billions  of pounds  have been spent over the last 20 to 30  years on diversity campaigns in schools  in corporations in the nhs in  institutions  and we've seen virtually no change at  all so i don't think focusing on the top  is going to get us to where we need to  go  you
 

